.TH MODF 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
modf, modff, modfl \- decompose floating-point number into integer and fractional parts
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double modf(double " x ", double *" iptr );
.br
.BI "float modff(float " x ", float *" iptr );
.br
.BI "long double modfl(long double " x ", long double *" iptr );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR modf ()
function breaks the argument
.I x
into its integral and fractional parts, each of which has the same
sign as
.IR x .
The integral part (truncated toward zero) is stored in the
.B double
pointed to by
.IR iptr ,
and the signed fractional part is returned.
.PP
The decomposition satisfies the identity
.PP
.RS 4
.IR x " == *" iptr " + " frac
.RE
.PP
where
.I frac
is the value returned by
.BR modf ().
The magnitude of the fractional part is strictly less than 1.
.PP
The
.BR modff ()
variant takes and returns
.B float
values; the
.BR modfl ()
variant takes and returns
.B "long double"
values.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR modf ()
returns the signed fractional part of
.IR x ,
with the same sign as
.IR x ,
and stores the signed integral part of
.I x
(truncated toward zero) in
.RI * iptr .
.PP
If
.I x
is +0 (\-0),
.BR modf ()
returns +0 (\-0) and stores +0 (\-0) in
.RI * iptr .
.PP
If
.I x
is positive infinity (negative infinity),
.BR modf ()
returns +0 (\-0) and stores positive infinity (negative infinity) in
.RI * iptr .
.PP
If
.I x
is a NaN,
.BR modf ()
returns a NaN and stores a NaN in
.RI * iptr .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR modf (),
.BR modff (),
.BR modfl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR modf (),
.BR modff (),
and
.BR modfl ()
conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The handling of special arguments
follows C99 Annex F.10.3 (IEC 60559).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program decomposes 3.14 into its integer and fractional
components and prints both:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double x = 3.14;
    double ipart, fpart;

    fpart = modf(x, &ipart);

    printf("x     = %.6f\en", x);
    printf("ipart = %.6f\en", ipart);
    printf("fpart = %.6f\en", fpart);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
x     = 3.140000
ipart = 3.000000
fpart = 0.140000
.EE
.RE
